isfied\",\"summary\":\"useradd -m -s /bin/bash appworker succeeded and id appworker confirms uid=1000(appworker). Account persisted after reboot as confirmed by post-restart id appworker output.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-55c3a00ede554554\",\"cmd-b545d0a93c3d4e09\",\"cmd-d6170633201c4adb\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Give appworker login sessions a limit of 8,192 open files (nofile)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"/etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf sets both soft and hard nofile to 8192. PAM pam_limits.so is required in system-auth and password-auth session stacks. Live verification via sudo -u appworker bash -c 'ulimit -n' returned 8192 both before and after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae\",\"cmd-04d8c07c9a09453c\",\"cmd-28baf8d54e644b5a\",\"cmd-d6170633201c4adb\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Give appworker login sessions a limit of 256 processes (nproc)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"/etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf sets both soft and hard nproc to 256. PAM pam_limits.so is required in session stacks. Live verification via sudo -u appworker bash -c 'ulimit -u' returned 256 both before and after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae\",\"cmd-04d8c07c9a09453c\",\"cmd-28baf8d54e644b5a\",\"cmd-d6170633201c4adb\"]}],\"confidence\":0.97,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands serve a clear task or protocol purpose: useradd created the required account, the limits.d write configured the required limits, and the reboot fulfilled the node-restart evidence protocol. One hygiene issue: /tmp/appworker-limits.conf (the staging file written in cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae before being copied to /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf) was not cleaned up and remains present in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot. This is low-risk residue but constitutes an unnecessary retention of a staging artifact. No unnecessary mutations were performed, no services were damaged, no security controls were weakened, and no conflicting configuration was introduced.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes are satisfied with strong evidence. The appworker account was created, /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf correctly sets nofile=8192 and nproc=256 (soft and hard), PAM pam_limits.so is active in the session stacks, and live ulimit verification as the appworker user confirmed both limits. The node-restart protocol is fully satisfied: pre-restart boot ID (19062a82…) was captured, shutdown issued, connection drop and polling observed, node returned online, and post-restart boot ID (8ad0f94b…) is distinct and corroborated by the lifecycle after_executor snapshot. Account and limits were confirmed intact post-reboot. Minor hygiene issue: the /tmp/appworker-limits.conf staging file was left behind after the reboot.\",\"limitations\":[\"The sudo -u appworker ulimit probe tests limits inherited from the invoking sudo session rather than a full PAM login session; however, pam_limits.so is confirmed active in both system-auth and password-auth, making the effective value highly reliable.\",\"The /tmp/appworker-limits.conf residue was observed in the after_executor snapshot but its content was not re-read post-reboot to confirm it survived (it is a /tmp file and may have been recreated or retained; either way it is harmless residue).\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create the appworker account on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"useradd -m -s /bin/bash appworker succeeded and id appworker confirms uid=1000(appworker). Account persisted after reboot as confirmed by post-restart id appworker output.","evidence_ids":["cmd-55c3a00ede554554","cmd-b545d0a93c3d4e09","cmd-d6170633201c4adb"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Give appworker login sessions a limit of 8,192 open files (nofile)","status":"satisfied","summary":"/etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf sets both soft and hard nofile to 8192. PAM pam_limits.so is required in system-auth and password-auth session stacks. Live verification via sudo -u appworker bash -c 'ulimit -n' returned 8192 both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae","cmd-04d8c07c9a09453c","cmd-28baf8d54e644b5a","cmd-d6170633201c4adb"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Give appworker login sessions a limit of 256 processes (nproc)","status":"satisfied","summary":"/etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf sets both soft and hard nproc to 256. PAM pam_limits.so is required in session stacks. Live verification via sudo -u appworker bash -c 'ulimit -u' returned 256 both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae","cmd-04d8c07c9a09453c","cmd-28baf8d54e644b5a","cmd-d6170633201c4adb"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"All state-changing commands serve a clear task or protocol purpose: useradd created the required account, the limits.d write configured the required limits, and the reboot fulfilled the node-restart evidence protocol. One hygiene issue: /tmp/appworker-limits.conf (the staging file written in cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae before being copied to /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf) was not cleaned up and remains present in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot. This is low-risk residue but constitutes an unnecessary retention of a staging artifact. No unnecessary mutations were performed, no services were damaged, no security controls were weakened, and no conflicting configuration was introduced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8b0a60bbdf6242ae","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes are satisfied with strong evidence. The appworker account was created, /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf correctly sets nofile=8192 and nproc=256 (soft and hard), PAM pam_limits.so is active in the session stacks, and live ulimit verification as the appworker user confirmed both limits. The node-restart protocol is fully satisfied: pre-restart boot ID (19062a82…) was captured, shutdown issued, connection drop and polling observed, node returned online, and post-restart boot ID (8ad0f94b…) is distinct and corroborated by the lifecycle after_executor snapshot. Account and limits were confirmed intact post-reboot. Minor hygiene issue: the /tmp/appworker-limits.conf staging file was left behind after the reboot.","limitations":["The sudo -u appworker ulimit probe tests limits inherited from the invoking sudo session rather than a full PAM login session; however, pam_limits.so is confirmed active in both system-auth and password-auth, making the effective value highly reliable.","The /tmp/appworker-limits.conf residue was observed in the after_executor snapshot but its content was not re-read post-reboot to confirm it survived (it is a /tmp file and may have been recreated or retained; either way it is harmless residue)."]},"ttft_ms":23774,"type":"result","duration_ms":41245,"uuid":"f5a3d7ae-13cc-4819-bf55-de7ad1d3cd41","ttft_stream_ms":1753,"time_to_request_ms":34,"queued_turn_count":0}
